WEBVTT

00:00:00.483 --> 00:00:06.122
PostgreSQL does not require a special PIVOT keyword for the core patterns taught here.

00:00:06.122 --> 00:00:09.327
You can use conditional aggregation.

00:00:09.327 --> 00:00:11.812
One common form is SUM with CASE.

00:00:11.812 --> 00:00:17.907
PostgreSQL also supports FILTER on aggregate functions, which can be very readable.

00:00:17.907 --> 00:00:25.052
Both approaches use the same underlying idea: one grouped output row with category-specific aggregate columns.

00:00:25.052 --> 00:00:29.902
A pivot converts category values into columns, usually through conditional aggregation in portable SQL.

00:00:29.902 --> 00:00:36.132
Each target column needs a clear aggregation rule.

00:00:36.132 --> 00:00:41.132
Before pivoting, check whether the source contains more than one value for the same row-key and category

00:00:41.132 --> 00:00:42.706
combination.
